1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:03,620
This planet has oceans, land, and a ring like Saturn.

2
00:00:03,640 --> 00:00:08,670
It sits in just the right spot for life. So is it a new world we just found?

3
00:00:08,780 --> 00:00:13,550
No. It's Earth, four hundred and sixty-six million years ago.

4
00:00:13,550 --> 00:00:19,380
So where did that ring come from, and where did it go? It starts with a rock that got too close.

5
00:00:19,410 --> 00:00:23,180
Back then, space rocks were hitting Earth far more often than today.

6
00:00:23,180 --> 00:00:27,690
One asteroid didn't hit us. It flew past, very close.

7
00:00:27,770 --> 00:00:33,160
Too close. Every planet has an invisible line around it, called the Roche limit.

8
00:00:33,210 --> 00:00:39,460
Cross it, and the planet pulls harder on your near side than your far side. Until you rip apart.

9
00:00:39,470 --> 00:00:45,800
The asteroid shattered into countless pieces. Over time, they spread into a ring around the equator.

10
00:00:45,810 --> 00:00:52,780
Earth looked like a small Saturn. And Saturn's rings may have started the same way, from something that got too close.

11
00:00:52,820 --> 00:00:57,530
So what would that look like from the ground? It depends on where you stand.

12
00:00:57,600 --> 00:01:03,370
On the equator, you'd see the ring edge-on. Just a thin bright line, straight overhead.

13
00:01:03,380 --> 00:01:08,050
Further north, it opens into a giant arch, stretching right across the sky.

14
00:01:08,050 --> 00:01:15,160
Near the poles, it sinks low, like a mountain made of light. Go far enough, and it drops below the horizon.

15
00:01:15,210 --> 00:01:20,250
At night, it would still catch sunlight. So nights would be a lot brighter.

16
00:01:20,250 --> 00:01:26,670
But rings cast shadows. In winter, a wide strip of Earth would sit in the shade, and get colder.

17
00:01:26,670 --> 00:01:32,360
About twenty million years later, Earth fell into one of its coldest periods in half a billion years.

18
00:01:32,390 --> 00:01:38,190
Ice sheets spread across the land. Around eighty-five percent of sea species died out.

19
00:01:38,190 --> 00:01:44,800
Some scientists think the ring's shadow helped cause it. It's still a new idea, and not everyone agrees.

20
00:01:44,960 --> 00:01:49,510
So how do we know the ring was there at all? Nobody took a photo.

21
00:01:49,520 --> 00:01:55,450
Scientists mapped the craters from that time. Normally, craters land all over a planet.

22
00:01:55,520 --> 00:02:02,590
But these twenty-one craters all sit in a band near the old equator. Exactly where a falling ring would drop its rocks.

23
00:02:02,700 --> 00:02:08,210
That also tells us where the ring went. Bit by bit, it rained back down on Earth.

24
00:02:08,240 --> 00:02:13,230
Because rings don't last forever. Even the rings of Saturn are falling apart.

25
00:02:13,290 --> 00:02:20,180
In 2017, the Cassini spacecraft flew between Saturn and its rings, and saw ring material raining onto the planet.

26
00:02:20,240 --> 00:02:24,150
In a few hundred million years, Saturn could lose its rings completely.

27
00:02:24,180 --> 00:02:30,650
A ringed Earth today would have one more problem. Rocks in the ring would smash into any satellite that crossed it.

28
00:02:30,650 --> 00:02:36,880
So could Earth get rings again? Only if something big crossed that line. Like the Moon.

29
00:02:36,940 --> 00:02:46,170
If the Moon came too close, it would break into a ring around us. But don't worry. The Moon is drifting away, about four centimetres a year.

30
00:02:46,200 --> 00:02:54,210
So tonight, look up. That plain sky once had a ring. And pieces of it may still be in the ground under your feet.
